{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Suppressing Chroma Spill.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Suppressing Chroma Spill.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"suppressing chroma spill suppressing chroma spill","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Suppressing Chroma Spill.md","fileName":"Suppressing Chroma Spill.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Suppressing Chroma Spill","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 18:17\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Compositing and Keying]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Compositing and Keying","Retaining Pristine RGB During Keying","digitalvisualeffectsandcompositing.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Suppressing Chroma Spill.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Suppressing Chroma Spill.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"suppressing chroma spill suppressing chroma spill","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Suppressing Chroma Spill.md","fileName":"Suppressing Chroma Spill.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Suppressing Chroma Spill","heading":"Suppressing Chroma Spill","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Light reflected from a blue or green screen can tint the subject, especially at its edges. Removing the screen in the alpha does not automatically remove this contamination from RGB. The book's blue-screen stereo example uses a hue correction to isolate and reduce blue spill after the initial key, then uses the alpha to retrieve the performer from original footage. Spill correction is therefore a color operation coordinated with, but separate from, [[Retaining Pristine RGB During Keying]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Compositing and Keying","Retaining Pristine RGB During Keying","digitalvisualeffectsandcompositing.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Suppressing Chroma Spill.md::